Q: Private Constructor in Python A: <p>If a module-level function is OK instead of a static method ...</p>
<p>Make the whole class private, and use a function to instantiate it. For added type safety, expose the API via an abstract class</p>
<pre><code>class Foo(ABC):
    @abstractmethod
    def bar(self) -&gt; int:
        ...


class _SpecificFoo(Foo):
    def __init__(self, bar: int):
        self._bar = bar

    def bar(self) -&gt; int:
        return self._bar


def specific_foo(bar: int) -&gt; Foo:
    return _SpecificFoo(bar)
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Note</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><code>_SpecificFoo()</code> and <code>specific_foo()</code> can have different  signatures</li>
<li><code>specific_foo()</code> returns a <code>Foo</code>. The API makes no mention of <code>_SpecificFoo</code></li>
</ul>
<p>This is very similar to Jesse's first option, but the class isn't redefined on each call to <code>specific_foo</code>, and the class interface is statically typed.</p>
<p>If you don't care for type hints, omit <code>Foo</code> everywhere.</p>

